  "notes": "UK-to-US uplift pop with gospel and turntable colour: 100–115 straight, backbeat on 2&4, with a 16th acoustic strum. The harmonic claim is the reason this model is not another I–V–vi–IV entry — the signature single is **modal**, F Mixolydian, \"a major tonic with a flat 7th; the harmonic loop is I–♭VII–IV, no dominant\" — so mixolydian is the top-weighted scale, the I–bVII–IV loop carries top weight, and no authored family contains a V anywhere. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"kick 1 and the '&' of 3\", \"16th hat with an open hat **before** the backbeat\" (which is why the open hat sits on \"1&\" and \"3&\" rather than the parent's \"2&\"/\"4&\"), and \"tambourine from chorus 1\"; the fourth stated fact, \"a full drum drop-out before the last chorus\", is `dropOutBeats` at 0.95 over 8 beats. The chorus instrument is a gospel choir \"answering the lead in block harmony\", which is the counter running sustain-pad and octave-echo at a moderate ratio, and the lead is \"syllable-dense and rhythmically spoken in the verse, wide and sustained in the chorus\" — a 4–10 density over a wide register. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium and marks the tempo **[LOW-CONF]**; `bpm.mode` 108 is this author's centre of the stated band. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are all drum probabilities and the bass numbers.",
